17. Key Findings

The analysis produced the following major findings:

Finding 1 — Dataset Quality

The dataset contains 80,000 records and 25 original variables, with missing values limited to the size column. No duplicate rows were identified.

Finding 2 — Pricing

The average product price was approximately ₹30,137, with a median of approximately ₹30,164.

Finding 3 — Discounts

The average discount was approximately 21.35%.

Average units sold remained relatively stable across the discount levels analyzed.

Finding 4 — Category Revenue

Toys had the highest estimated revenue at approximately ₹606.45B, followed by Beauty at approximately ₹605.82B.

Finding 5 — Seller Performance

MegaStore recorded the highest average product score at approximately 51.07.

Finding 6 — Ratings and Sales

The rating-to-sales correlation was approximately -0.0012, indicating almost no linear relationship.

Finding 7 — Delivery and Sales

Delivery days had a correlation of approximately 0.0016 with units sold, indicating an extremely weak linear relationship.

Finding 8 — Return Policy and Sales

Return-policy duration had a correlation of approximately -0.0039 with units sold.

Finding 9 — High-Rated Low-Sales Products

The analysis identified 160 products with ratings above 4 but fewer than 50 units sold.

18. Business Recommendations
18.1 Avoid Blanket Discounting

Since average units sold remain relatively similar across discount levels, discounting alone should not be treated as the primary driver of sales.

Recommendation: Evaluate discounts together with price, category, product score, seller, and sales volume.

18.2 Investigate High-Rated Low-Sales Products

The 160 products with ratings above 4 and fewer than 50 units sold represent potential products for further investigation.

Recommendation: Examine their pricing, discounts, seller performance, stock availability, and product categories to identify possible visibility or positioning opportunities.

18.3 Monitor High-Revenue Categories

Toys and Beauty produced the highest estimated category revenue.

Recommendation: These categories can be monitored closely for inventory, product assortment, and promotional opportunities.

18.4 Evaluate Sellers Using Multiple Metrics

MegaStore had the highest average product score, but seller performance should not be judged by this metric alone.

Recommendation: Combine product score with seller rating, units sold, delivery performance, and product ratings for a more complete evaluation.

18.5 Do Not Assume Ratings Guarantee Sales

The almost-zero rating-to-sales correlation indicates that rating alone is not a strong linear indicator of units sold within this dataset.

Recommendation: Consider multiple product attributes when analyzing sales performance.

19. Limitations

This analysis has several limitations based on the variables available in the notebook:

revenue_estimate is calculated rather than taken from actual transaction revenue.
Correlation measures linear association and does not establish causation.
The analysis does not include actual profit or margin.
The notebook does not establish why certain products have high or low sales.
Factors not represented in the dataset may also influence sales.
The high-rating/low-sales analysis identifies products for investigation but does not establish the reason for their low sales.
20. Conclusion

This project demonstrates an end-to-end Python-based exploratory analysis of 80,000 Flipkart product records.

The analysis began with data-quality assessment and missing-value treatment, followed by feature engineering and descriptive statistics. It then examined product sales, category-level estimated revenue, discount behavior, seller performance, customer ratings, delivery time, return policies, product weight, and potentially underperforming products.

The analysis found that Toys had the highest estimated category revenue, while discount levels showed relatively similar average units sold. The correlation analysis also showed extremely weak linear relationships between units sold and rating, delivery time, return-policy duration, and product weight.

Overall, the project demonstrates the use of Python for data cleaning, feature engineering, exploratory data analysis, statistical analysis, visualization, and business-oriented interpretation.

